**user**:
> How can I replace all instances of "0.36" in a directory tree of R files with "0.53"?
**assistant**:
> You can use the `sed` command in a Unix-like terminal to recursively search through all R files in a directory and replace "0.36" with "0.53". Here's how you can do it:
> 
> 1. Open your terminal.
> 2. Navigate to the directory where you want to perform the replacement.
> 3. Run the following command:
> 
>    ```bash
>    find . -type f -name "*.R" -exec sed -i '' 's/0\.36/0\.53/g' {} +
>    ```
> 
> This command does the following:
> - `find . -type f -name "*.R"` finds all files in the current directory (and subdirectories) with the `.R` extension.
> - `-exec ... {} +` executes the `sed` command on each file found.
> - `sed -i '' 's/0\.36/0\.53/g'` edits the files in-place globally (`g`) for all occurrences. Make sure to escape the dot (`.`) as it is a special character in regex.
> 
> **Important Note:** This operation is not reversible, so you may want to back up your files first. Alternatively, you can modify the `sed` command to create backup files by changing `-i ''` to `-i.backup`, which will save the original files with a `.backup` extension.
